COPYING: the licenses of this repository
========================================

This file is information about how this repository is licensed. It is
written to be helpful. It is NOT LEGAL ADVICE, it does not grant,
change or limit any license, and if it differs from the text of a
license, the text of the license is what counts. If it matters to you
what you may do, ask your own counsel.

The texts of the licenses are in the files named LICENSE-*, which hold
nothing but the license, so that they can be told by tools. There are
three, and the sections below say what each covers.

Runtimes: MIT (LICENSE-RUNTIMES)
--------------------------------

The runtimes, the libraries that load compiled specifications and
encode and decode with them:

- packages/asnkitrt, the runtime for Python.
- packages/asnkitrt-rs, the runtime for Rust, with its tests, its
  fuzz targets and the certificates it is tried with.
- packages/asnkitrt-wasm, the runtime for the web and JavaScript, with
  its tests.
- Runtimes in other languages in this repository.

Runtimes in other languages, in other repositories, are licensed under
the same license. Each runtime folder has a copy of the license, with
the same name, which is the one built into its package.

SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

Documentation and corpus: CC-BY-4.0 (LICENSE-DOCUMENTATION)
-----------------------------------------------------------

The documentation of the formats and the files for testing a reader
of them, so that anyone can use them in an implementation of the
formats, of any license:

- docs/file_formats.md, the specification of the formats.
- examples/corpus, the corpus of .asnc files, with its keys and its
  README.
- tests/vectors/README.rst, the description of the test vectors.
  The test vectors themselves are not under this license: see the
  next section.

The code in them is not under this license but under MIT
(LICENSE-RUNTIMES), so that it can be copied into a program. That is
the code blocks of the specification, make.sh and foo.asn in the
corpus, and record.py and values.py, which make the test vectors.

Attribution, as the license asks for it: Copyright (c) 2025 BoxTheta,
the work is called asnkit, and its home is
https://github.com/boxtheta/asnkit. Parts derive from asn1tools by
Erik Moqvist (see NOTICE).

SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 AND MIT

Test vectors: under their own licenses
--------------------------------------

The test vectors are included in this repository under their own
licenses, not under the licenses of this repository. They are:

- tests/vectors/specs, the compiled forms of ASN.1 modules written by
  other parties (3GPP, ETSI, IETF, OMA, the authors of asn1tools and
  others), taken from tests/files.
- tests/vectors/vectors.jsonl.gz, the values encoded and decoded with
  those modules, which are made from them.

Each module, and what is made from it, stays under the license and the
terms of its own authors. BoxTheta does not license them: none of the
LICENSE-* files applies to them. Whoever uses, copies or redistributes them is responsible for
checking those terms. The same holds for the modules in tests/files
that come from other parties, and for their compiled forms in
tests/fixtures.

Of tests/vectors, only README.rst (CC-BY-4.0), and record.py and
values.py (MIT), which BoxTheta wrote, are under the licenses of this
repository, as the section on the documentation and the corpus says.

The compiler and everything else: Apache-2.0 (LICENSE-COMPILER)
---------------------------------------------------------------

The compiler, packages/asnkit-compiler and packages/asnkitcc,
the plugins, such as packages/asnkit-gen-python and packages/asnkit-gen-rust,
and everything else in this
repository that the sections above do not name: the tests, the other
examples, the other documentation and the test files, but for what is
under its own license as the section above says.

SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

What the compiler and the plugins make
--------------------------------------

The compiled specifications (.asnc files), the syntax trees (.asnst
files) and what the plugins write, such as the Python code of
asnkit-gen-python and the Rust code of asnkit-gen-rust, are for you to
use in your own programs under terms of your choice. The Apache
License does not cover what a program makes when it is run, and what
you give the compiler as input stays under your terms.

A plugin also copies some of its own code into what it writes, as the
helper code of the Python or Rust module. That code is not under the
Apache License either: the header of each file a plugin of this
repository writes says that the file may be used under terms of your
choice, so it does not need to come with a copy of the license or a
notice.

This is for the output only. The compiler and the plugins, and changes
to them, stay under the Apache License as the section above says.

A plugin by someone else has the license its author gives it, and what
that plugin makes is under what its author says.

Headers in the files
--------------------

The code files (Python and shell) start with a header of their
copyright and an SPDX-License-Identifier, which is the license that
covers that file. The header of a file that derives from asn1tools has
the copyright of Erik Moqvist as well (not the Rust files, which are
rewrites). Other files, such as the ASN.1
files, have no header and are covered as the sections above tell.
Files in tests/files taken from standards and other organizations keep
the terms they came with, which this repository does not change.

Commercial support
------------------

BoxTheta offers commercial support for asnkit, such as help with
integrating it, priority fixes and development of features. Ask
sales@boxtheta.com for a quote. It is not needed to use asnkit under
the licenses above.

Paying for support helps keep the open source version maintained.

Code from asn1tools
-------------------

Parts of this repository derive from asn1tools by Erik Moqvist and its
contributors, which was released under the MIT License. Its text and
copyright notice are in NOTICE and have to stay with that code. They
are part of what is built into each package.

